Guided Exercise: Deploy Applications from an Image and from a Template
Goal of the Exercise
Learn two ways to deploy applications in OpenShift:
1️⃣ From a Template
2️⃣ From a Container Image
Then compare the resources created by each method.
________________________________________
Step 1 — Prepare the Lab Environment
Command:
lab start deploy-newapp
What it does
This command:
•	Starts the lab environment
•	Connects to the OpenShift cluster
•	Prepares required resources
________________________________________
Step 2 — Login to OpenShift
Command:
oc login -u developer -p developer https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
Explanation
Part	Meaning
oc	OpenShift CLI
login	authenticate
-u developer	username
-p developer	password
URL	OpenShift API server
After login:
Login successful
________________________________________
Step 3 — Create a New Project
Command:
oc new-project deploy-newapp
What is a Project?
In OpenShift:
Project = Kubernetes Namespace + access control
It is used to:
•	isolate applications
•	organize resources
•	control permissions
After creation:
Now using project "deploy-newapp"
OpenShift automatically switches to that project.
________________________________________
Step 4 — Verify the Project is Empty
Command:
oc get pods
Output:
No resources found
Meaning:
There are no pods running yet.
________________________________________
Step 5 — Check Default Resources
Command:
oc get serviceaccounts,secrets
Example output:
serviceaccount/builder
serviceaccount/default
serviceaccount/deployer
Service Accounts
Used by pods to:
•	access the cluster
•	pull images
•	deploy applications
________________________________________
Secrets
Secrets store sensitive data like:
•	registry credentials
•	tokens
•	passwords
Example:
secret/default-dockercfg
Used to pull container images from registry.
________________________________________
Step 6 — Inspect the Template
Command:
oc describe template mysql-persistent -n openshift
Explanation
Part	Meaning
describe	show detailed info
template mysql-persistent	template name
-n openshift	namespace where template exists
________________________________________
Resources Created by Template
The template contains these resources:
Secret
Service
PersistentVolumeClaim
Deployment
Meaning:
When template runs it creates:
Resource	Purpose
Secret	store database credentials
Service	network access
PVC	persistent storage
Deployment	manage pods
________________________________________
Step 7 — Deploy Database Using Template
Command:
oc new-app --name mysql \
--template mysql-persistent \
-l team=red \
-p MYSQL_USER=developer \
-p MYSQL_PASSWORD=developer
________________________________________
Explanation of Command
Option	Meaning
new-app	create application
--name mysql	app name
--template mysql-persistent	template used
-l team=red	attach label
-p	template parameter
Parameters used:
MYSQL_USER=developer
MYSQL_PASSWORD=developer
________________________________________
Resources Created
Output:
secret "mysql" created
service "mysql" created
persistentvolumeclaim "mysql" created
deployment.apps "mysql" created
So template created 4 resources automatically.
________________________________________
Step 8 — Watch Pod Creation
Command:
watch oc get pods
What watch does
Runs command repeatedly every few seconds.
Example output:
mysql-8d7d996f-qn798   Running
Meaning:
Deployment created a pod.
Stop with:
Ctrl + C
________________________________________
Step 9 — Deploy Database Using Container Image
Now create another database using only an image.
Command:
oc new-app --name db-image -l team=blue \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel9/mysql-80:1 \
-e MYSQL_USER=developer \
-e MYSQL_PASSWORD=developer \
-e MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=redhat
________________________________________
Command Explanation
Option	Meaning
--name db-image	application name
-l team=blue	attach label
--image	container image
-e	environment variable
Environment variables configure MySQL:
MYSQL_USER
MYSQL_PASSWORD
MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD
________________________________________
Resources Created
Output:
imagestream "db-image"
deployment "db-image"
service "db-image"
So image deployment creates:
Resource	Purpose
ImageStream	track container images
Deployment	manage pods
Service	networking
Notice:
❌ No Secret
❌ No PVC
________________________________________
Step 10 — Watch Both Pods
Command:
watch oc get pods -L team
Example output:
NAME                STATUS   TEAM
db-image-xxxxx      Running  blue
mysql-xxxxx         Running
Observation:
Only db-image pod has label.
Why?
Because template did not include label in pod template.
________________________________________
Step 11 — Check Readiness Probe
Template pod:
oc get pods -l deployment=mysql \
-o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.containers[0].readinessProbe}' | jq
Output:
mysqladmin ping
What is Readiness Probe?
A readiness probe checks:
Is container ready to accept traffic?
Example check:
mysqladmin ping
________________________________________
Image-based pod
Command:
oc get pods -l deployment=db-image \
-o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.containers[0].readinessProbe}'
Output:
(empty)
Meaning:
❌ No readiness probe.
________________________________________
Step 12 — Check Resource Limits
Template pod:
oc get pods -l deployment=mysql \
-o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.containers[0].resources.limits}'
Output:
memory: 512Mi
Meaning:
Memory limit = 512 MB
________________________________________
Image-based pod:
{}
Meaning:
❌ No resource limits.
________________________________________
Step 13 — Check Secrets
Command:
oc get secrets
Output:
mysql   Opaque
Meaning:
Template created secret for database credentials.
Image-based deployment did not create secret.
________________________________________
Step 14 — View Services
Command:
oc get services
Output:
db-image
mysql
Both applications have services.
________________________________________
Step 15 — Filter Resources with Labels
Command:
oc get services -l team=red
Output:
mysql
Meaning:
Shows only resources with label:
team=red
________________________________________
Step 16 — Delete Template Resources
Command:
oc delete all -l team=red
Deletes:
•	service
•	deployment
•	pods
But does not delete:
•	secrets
•	PVC
________________________________________
Step 17 — Delete Remaining Resources
Command:
oc delete secret,pvc -l team=red
Deletes:
•	secret
•	persistent volume claim
________________________________________
Step 18 — Verify Deletion
Command:
oc get secret,svc,pvc,deployment -l team=red
Output:
No resources found
Template resources deleted.
________________________________________
Step 19 — Verify Image Deployment Still Exists
Command:
oc get is,deployment,svc
Output:
imagestream/db-image
deployment/db-image
service/db-image
Image-based application still running.
________________________________________
Final Comparison
Feature	Template Deployment	Image Deployment
Secret	✅ Yes	❌ No
Service	✅ Yes	✅ Yes
Deployment	✅ Yes	✅ Yes
PVC	✅ Yes	❌ No
ImageStream	❌ No	✅ Yes
Readiness Probe	✅ Yes	❌ No
Resource Limits	✅ Yes	❌ No
________________________________________
Key Concept
Template Deployment
More production-ready because it includes:
•	secrets
•	storage
•	probes
•	limits
________________________________________
Image Deployment
More basic deployment.
Creates only:
•	imagestream
•	deployment
•	service
________________________________________
Lab Finish
Command:
lab finish deploy-newapp
This:
•	cleans resources
•	resets environment

